@startuml Decorator-Middleware-Objects
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
title Order is part of the design — the same decorators, different chains, different numbers

package "logging outside the retry" {
  object "aLoggingPriceFeed" as l1
  object "aRetryingPriceFeed" as r1
  object "theSupplier" as s1
  l1 --> r1 : inner
  r1 --> s1 : inner
}
note bottom of s1
  1 failure, then success
  **2 log lines** — "asking", "got"
  The retry is invisible in the log.
  2 supplier calls.
end note

package "logging inside the retry" {
  object "aRetryingPriceFeed " as r2
  object "aLoggingPriceFeed " as l2
  object "theSupplier " as s2
  r2 --> l2 : inner
  l2 --> s2 : inner
}
note bottom of s2
  1 failure, then success
  **4 log lines** — a pair per attempt,
  the failure among them.
  2 supplier calls.
end note

package "timing outside the cache" {
  object "aTimingPriceFeed" as t3
  object "aCachingPriceFeed" as c3
  object "theSupplier  " as s3
  t3 --> c3 : inner
  c3 --> s3 : inner
}
note bottom of s3
  2 requests, 1 miss + 1 hit
  **2 samples**: 200 ms and 0 ms.
  "What the caller waited for."
end note

package "timing inside the cache" {
  object "aCachingPriceFeed " as c4
  object "aTimingPriceFeed " as t4
  object "theSupplier   " as s4
  c4 --> t4 : inner
  t4 --> s4 : inner
}
note bottom of s4
  2 requests, 1 miss + 1 hit
  **1 sample**: 200 ms.
  "What the supplier itself cost."
  The hit never reached the timer.
end note

note as N1
  Nothing in the type system distinguishes these chains.
  Both compile, both run, both look right in review.
  A dashboard that silently moved from one timing chain to
  the other would report a 50% improvement and mean nothing.

  Not every pair differs: cache and retry give the same
  numbers in either order here, because failures are not
  cached. **Reason about each pair — do not recite a rule.**
end note

@enduml
